After some research and testing, I finally found the source of the problem.

To put things in context, I was having an issue with the streaming functionality of my dev environment. The setup includes a dev container behind an Nginx server on a VPS. I was able to receive the OpenAI response in the chat, but it came all at once, rather than in a streamed manner as expected.

The root of the problem turned out to be the **buffering in Nginx**. Once I disabled it, the streaming functionality was restored. However, it's worth noting that disabling buffering might have an impact on performance.

Here's how to disable it:

```config
server {
    listen 80;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:3000;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection 'upgrade';
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_cache_bypass $http_upgrade;

        # Disable buffering
        proxy_buffering off;
    }
}
```

That said, there might be a better way to disable buffering for specific routes. But, it could require synchronization between the Nginx configuration and the routes managed by Next.js, which would be more a question of organization.

I appreciate the guidance and the tips. Thanks, @EduardoFazolo for pointing me in the right direction.

