I started working on a PR to fix this problem and, as I was updating the documentation, discovered that the default provider chain won't run in browser, which would likely break things for anyone using this library in a client side component.

In reading AWS' documentation on the [Node Credential Providers](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSJavaScriptSDK/v3/latest/Package/-aws-sdk-credential-providers/#fromNodeProviderChain), I realized there is a simple workaround to support any authentication needs using the `options.bedrockOptions.credentials` configuration. For anyone else that runs across this issue, here's a code snippet that is working perfectly for our server-side execution:

```typescript
import { generateText } from 'ai';
import { createAmazonBedrock } from '@ai-sdk/amazon-bedrock';
import { fromNodeProviderChain } from '@aws-sdk/credential-providers';

const bedrock = createAmazonBedrock({
  bedrockOptions: {
    region: 'us-east-1',
    // tells BedrockRuntimeClient to use the default NodeJS provider chain, which will
    // load AWS profiles, Container Roles, Instance Roles, etc.
    credentials: fromNodeProviderChain(),
  },
});

const { text } = await generateText({
  model: bedrock('anthropic.claude-3-5-sonnet-20240620-v1:0'),
  prompt: 'Please explain the order in which the default AWS Credential Chain looks up credentials.',
});
console.log(text);
```

