We're seeing a similar issue on a client-rendered application using `@ai-sdk/react` versions 1.1.10 to 1.1.18 (inclusive), running on 1.1.9 works just fine.

Our component where `useChat()` is invoked also contains a `useEffect`, like so:

```ts
const { messages, /* other stuff */ } = useChat({ id, initialMessages });
const { parsedMessages, parseMessages } = useMessageParser();
useEffect(() => parseMessages(messages), [messages, parseMessages]);

// ...remaining component
```

One workaround was to use `messages.length` in the `useEffect` dependencies array, fine, but it seems off, so I kept digging.

I thought perhaps we were doing something funky in our message parser within `useMessageParser()`, perhaps mutating the state from another `useSWR()` hook using the same key, so I removed our parser and just did this:

```ts
const initialMessages = useMemo(() => [/* some hardcoded messages */], []);
const { messages } = useChat({ id, initialMessages });
const [,increment] = useState(0);
useEffect(() => increment((prev) => prev + 1), [messages]);

return false; // empty react component
```

This reproduces our infinite render. It appears that if the component re-renders due to a state-change (outside of `useChat`), `messages` returned by `useChat` is a new array even though it's contents haven't changed. If `useChat` properly memoized the `messages` array, the above example would only call `increment()` once.

